---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import WebhookValidator from '../../components/WebhookValidator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Webhook Payload Validator" description="Check an incoming webhook payload against your expected field list and types.">
  <WebhookValidator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why webhook payloads deserve validation just like API responses</h2>
  <p>It's tempting to trust a webhook payload blindly since it "came from a known provider," but providers occasionally change payload shape, and validating structure before processing catches integration-breaking changes early instead of causing a confusing downstream failure.</p>

  <h2>How to use this validator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Define the fields and types you expect in the payload.</li>
    <li>Paste an actual webhook payload.</li>
    <li>See which expected fields are present, missing, or wrong-typed.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming a provider's payload shape never changes</strong> — APIs and webhook payloads do evolve, sometimes without much advance notice.</li>
    <li><strong>Not handling missing optional fields gracefully</strong>, causing errors when a provider omits a field that's genuinely optional in their spec.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Testing whether your expected field assumptions match a provider's actual payload structure</li>
    <li>Debugging a webhook handler that's failing on an unexpected payload shape</li>
    <li>Documenting the expected schema for a webhook your own system sends</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this validate against a provider's official schema?</strong> No — you define the expected fields yourself based on documentation or observed payloads; it checks your payload against your own expectations, not an external schema registry.</p>
</ToolLayout>
